## Summary

`before_tool_call` plugin hooks never run for a call the harness's own tool-loop detector blocks at **critical** tier. `runBeforeToolCallHook()` returns its veto (`blocked: true`) immediately after `detectToolCallLoop()` reports `level: "critical"` — before `getGlobalHookRunner()` (the function that dispatches to every registered `before_tool_call` plugin hook) is ever called. A plugin-owned loop guard that would give the model real, actionable, directive guidance instead of the harness's static template text is registered correctly, fires correctly on every other call shape, and simply never executes for this one — not a race, not a plugin bug, a hard `return` upstream of the plugin hook subsystem.

Reproduced independently twice, on two different agents, five nights apart: 2026-08-22 (single repeated-call `generic_repeat` shape) and 2026-08-27 (a sustained two-call `ping_pong` alternation). Both end the same way: the harness's raw, non-actionable template text repeats for the rest of the run, the plugin's directive replacement text never appears once, and the session eventually aborts with no final reply, burning the full run-budget timeout.

## Root cause

Traced in the installed `openclaw` package (`openclaw@2026.7.1-2`), `agent-tools.before-tool-call-C95DXQXZ.js`, `runBeforeToolCallHook()`:

```js
async function runBeforeToolCallHook(args) {
	const toolName = normalizeToolName(args.toolName || "tool");
	const params = args.params;
	try {
		if (args.ctx?.sessionKey) {
			const { getDiagnosticSessionState, logToolLoopAction, detectToolCallLoop, recordToolCall } = await loadBeforeToolCallRuntime();
			const sessionState = getDiagnosticSessionState({ sessionKey: args.ctx.sessionKey, sessionId: args.ctx.sessionId });
			const loopScope = args.ctx.runId ? { runId: args.ctx.runId } : void 0;
			const loopResult = detectToolCallLoop(sessionState, toolName, params, args.ctx.loopDetection, loopScope);
			if (loopResult.stuck) {
				if (loopResult.level === "critical") {
					log.error(`Blocking ${toolName} due to critical loop: ${loopResult.message}`);
					logToolLoopAction({ /* ... */ });
					return {
						blocked: true,
						kind: "veto",
						deniedReason: "tool-loop",
						reason: loopResult.message,
						params
					};
				}
				// (warning-level path continues below, does NOT return early)
			}
			if (args.ctx.loopDetection?.enabled !== false) recordToolCall(sessionState, toolName, params, args.toolCallId, args.ctx.loopDetection, loopScope);
		}
		const hookRunner = getGlobalHookRunner();
		const hasBeforeToolCallHooks = hookRunner?.hasHooks("before_tool_call") === true;
		// ... plugin before_tool_call hooks are invoked from here down ...
```

(exact line numbers in the installed build: the critical-tier `return` is at 1551–1557; `getGlobalHookRunner()` is not called until line 1577.)

`getGlobalHookRunner()` is what dispatches to every registered plugin `before_tool_call` handler. The critical-tier branch returns *before that line is ever reached*. This is unconditional — it does not depend on which detector fired. Checked all four branches in `tool-loop-detection-QRwz_WH8.js` directly: `unknown_tool_repeat`, `known_poll_no_progress`, `ping_pong`, and `global_circuit_breaker` all set `level: "critical"` and therefore all hit the same early return. A plugin cannot distinguish or intercept any of them — it simply never runs.

This compounds #127701 (critical-tier blocks never reach `recordToolCall`, so the detector's own count freezes) but is a distinct mechanism: even with #127701 fixed and the circuit breaker properly reachable, `global_circuit_breaker`'s result is *also* `level: "critical"`, so it *also* hits this same early return. Fixing #127701 changes how the session eventually ends (faster abort via circuit breaker instead of a full run-budget timeout); it does not make this call reachable by any plugin hook.

## Reproductions

Both incidents show the identical signature: the harness's raw template text repeats verbatim for every subsequent retry, a project-owned plugin `before_tool_call` hook exists specifically to replace that text with actionable guidance on exactly this pattern, and it never fires — confirmed by exact string match against the raw vendor templates, not inferred.

| # | Date | Agent | Detector(s) | Evidence | Plugin directive text observed? | Final outcome |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | 2026-08-22 | Skeptic | `generic_repeat` | `test-skeptic-qwen38-ceiling2000-1787302688.jsonl`, entries 245–259 | No — 0 of ~100 blocked retries | `stopReason: "aborted"`, `content: []`, `errorMessage: "This operation was aborted"`; trajectory `session.ended`: `status: "error"`, `timedOutByRunBudget: true` |
| 2 | 2026-08-27 | Valuation | `ping_pong` + `generic_repeat` (alternating) | `zzvalpty9-live-1787740774.jsonl`, events 31–131 | No — 0 of 26 consecutive `CRITICAL` blocks | `stopReason: "aborted"`, `content: []`, `usage: {0,0,0}`; `openclaw:prompt-error`, `"error": "request timed out"` |

Reproduction 2's plugin gate has a stated trigger condition (the just-blocked tool name matches the next attempted tool name) that is textually satisfied on nearly every transition in this transcript — both calls in the alternation use the same `exec` tool, just with different arguments — yet its replacement text still never appears once, confirming this is not a plugin-logic gap on the reporter's side; the harness's own control flow never gives the hook a chance to run at all.

Independently confirmed the plugin's `before_tool_call` hooks genuinely fire elsewhere in the same session (a different, unrelated project-owned gate's directive text appears correctly earlier in the same transcript) — ruling out a plugin-registration or session-scoping explanation.

## Also checked and ruled out: `before_message_write` as an after-the-fact workaround

Before filing, checked whether a plugin could instead detect this pattern after the fact and rewrite the final reply via `before_message_write`. Confirmed dead for the same underlying reason in both incidents: the session never produces a final assistant reply to rewrite. Both end `stopReason: "aborted"` with empty `content` — the model burns the full run-budget timeout retrying (or alternating between) blocked calls and the run is torn down with no message ever written. There is nothing downstream for `before_message_write` to intercept. Saving anyone who finds this issue the trouble of proposing that as a workaround.

## Why this matters

A plugin can correctly detect a stuck pattern, register a `before_tool_call` hook specifically to redirect it with real, actionable, directive guidance, and that hook will be silently skipped for exactly the calls it most needs to intercept — any call the harness's own detector also independently vetoes at critical tier, which is the common case for a sustained loop, since the harness's own detector keeps re-firing on every subsequent repeat of the pattern it already flagged. The net effect: plugin-level loop mitigation is structurally unreachable for the one class of call (a harness-vetoed critical loop) it would matter most for. The only way a plugin can currently intervene is on a *different* call than the one just blocked — e.g. the next dispatch in a *new* session, which is a real but much narrower mitigation than in-session redirection.

## Possible directions (not a PR)

Not prescribing a specific fix, but two shapes seem plausible from the control flow above:

1. Run the plugin `before_tool_call` hook chain (`getGlobalHookRunner()`) *before* the critical-tier early return, so a plugin gets a chance to act (e.g. supply its own `blocked`/`reason`) even when the harness's own detector would also veto the call — letting a plugin's more actionable message take precedence over the generic template.
2. Have the critical-tier veto path itself dispatch through the plugin hook chain (e.g. pass the harness's own veto result into `before_tool_call` hooks as context, allowing a hook to override `reason` without needing to intercept before the veto exists).

Either would let a project-owned guard's directive text actually reach the model for the failure mode it's built to handle, instead of being silently bypassed by the very veto that triggers it.

## Related

- #127701 (critical-tier tool-loop block prevents its own escalation) is related but distinct: that issue is about the harness's own *detection/escalation* mechanism failing to advance its own counter. This issue is about *plugin reachability*: even with #127701 fixed, the plugin hook chain still never runs for a critical-tier veto, because `global_circuit_breaker` (the detector #127701 makes reachable) is *also* `level: "critical"` and hits the identical early return traced above.
- #127807 (no plugin-facing hook can force agent-run termination) is also related but distinct: that issue is about a plugin correctly detecting and blocking a stuck pattern but having no way to *end the turn/session* once it has. This issue is a layer earlier — the plugin's `before_tool_call` hook does not even get *invoked* for a harness-vetoed critical call, so a plugin cannot even supply its own denial reason, let alone terminate anything.

## Environment

- `openclaw` npm package version: `2026.7.1-2`
- Reproduced against a local Ollama provider (`qwen3.8:27b`) on both incidents, but the bug is in the hook-dispatch control flow itself and is not provider-specific.
- Files referenced: `agent-tools.before-tool-call-C95DXQXZ.js` (`runBeforeToolCallHook`), `tool-loop-detection-QRwz_WH8.js` (`detectToolCallLoop`, all four critical-tier detector branches).

### Bug type

Behavior bug (incorrect output/state without crash)

### Beta release blocker

No

### Summary

I stopped a long Telegram DM run with `/stop` while messages were queued. The oldest queued message started as a follow-up 40 ms after the abort and wrote its user row to the transcript. About 2.8 s later, after the stop reply was delivered, the fast-abort path mirrored the `/stop` exchange into the transcript: a `command-input:` user row and a `command-reply:` assistant row. When the follow-up reached replay admission, the current turn was the `/stop` row, and admission failed with `Persisted user turn changed before replay admission`.

On 2026.10.10 the failure then went through model fallback and ended with `Session transcript keyed user is outside the current turn: <id>`. The user got the generic "OpenClaw couldn't finish this reply". The queued message is in the transcript once, unanswered, and nothing retries it. This happened at both `/stop`s that day.

A unit test reproduces the refusal on main `54ed2bcc74b`, on v2026.11.1 and on 2026.10.10. The same test replays fine when the interposed row is a Workshop notice, which #181473 fixed. The `/stop` row is a user row, so the refresh-once still refuses it.

A second point, on 2026.10.10 only: the `2026.10.2` notes say "`/stop` clears queued follow-ups as well as active work, so waiting messages do not immediately restart the agent". Here the oldest queued message did immediately restart the agent, and the newer ones were dropped. My read is that the drain had already taken the head item in flight (`utils/queue-helpers.ts:229`) and Stop's cleanup skips in-flight items (`auto-reply/reply/queue/cleanup.ts:111-114`). Main rewrote Stop and the queue in #175837, and I have not tested whether main still lets it through. The replay half applies to any turn admitted between the abort and the mirror.

### Steps to reproduce

Unit test, attached below. Main version: `src/agents/embedded-agent-runner/run/attempt-session-replay.stop-exchange.test.ts`, added to `test/vitest/vitest.database-worker-core-paths.mjs` next to `attempt-session-replay.test.ts`. Run it with `node scripts/test-projects.mts <file>`.

1. Build a keyed persisted user turn with the existing `withInterruptedTurn` / `withReplaySession` fixture.
2. After replay preparation and before admission, call `recordDeliveredCommandExchange({ commandText: "/stop", ... })`. That is the call the fast-abort mirror makes (`dispatch-from-config.transcript.ts:53-67`).
3. Submit.

Results on main `54ed2bcc74b`, both replay windows:

- With nothing interposed: 1 model call, answered.
- With a Workshop notice interposed: 1 model call, answered.
- With the `/stop` exchange interposed: throws `Persisted user turn changed before replay admission. Retry the turn.`, 0 model calls, 1 user row, 1 `command-input` row.

v2026.11.1 gives the same result. 2026.10.10 throws `Persisted user turn changed before replay admission`.

Live sequence:

1. A long turn is running, and one or more messages are queued as follow-ups (steering rejected).
2. Send `/stop`.

### Expected behavior

Either `/stop` discards the queued message, as the 2026.10.2 notes say, or the message runs and gets an answer. A stop that happened before a turn was admitted should not invalidate that turn. Possible fixes, in order of preference:

- Write the stop's command exchange before the session is released to the next turn, so the transcript order matches what happened.
- Or treat a `command-input:` / `command-reply:` pair committed by a stop that preceded the turn's admission as traversable in the current-turn walk.
- Or re-queue the persisted turn once when admission fails this way.

### Actual behavior

Transcript rows (kinds only), 2026-11-05 EST:

