### Bug type

Behavior bug (incorrect output/state without crash)

### Beta release blocker

No

### Summary

In a Gateway session, a safeguard compaction whose cut lands on a user message is treated as a split turn. The complete turn before the cut is then summarized a second time as a "turn prefix". Each such compaction makes two summary requests instead of one, and the summary carries a finished turn in its split-turn section.

The cause is the `openclaw:bootstrap-context:full` entry that the embedded runner appends after every completed run ([`attempt-finalize.ts`](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/1ad3ec3d2081f3c7228b5bed8ae95e3821cd609f/src/agents/embedded-agent-runner/run/attempt-finalize.ts#L315)). It sits right before the next user message. `findCutPoint` moves the cut onto that metadata entry ([`compaction-cut-point.ts` L288-L307](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/1ad3ec3d2081f3c7228b5bed8ae95e3821cd609f/packages/agent-core/src/harness/compaction/compaction-cut-point.ts#L288-L307)). Then `isTurnStartEntry(cutEntry)` checks the metadata entry, which has no message, and `findTurnStartIndex` returns the previous turn's user message.

Found while validating #194813. That PR does not touch this code.

### Steps to reproduce

**Unit level.** This test fails on `main` at `1ad3ec3d2`. The control case without the marker passes.

```ts
import { describe, expect, it } from "vitest";
import type { AgentMessage } from "../../types.js";
import type { SessionTreeEntry } from "../types.js";
import { findCutPoint } from "./compaction.js";
import { assistant, text, user } from "./shard-fixtures.test-support.js";

const at = (index: number) => ({ id: `entry-${index}`, parentId: index === 0 ? null : `entry-${index - 1}`, timestamp: new Date(index + 1).toISOString() });
const message = (msg: AgentMessage, index: number): SessionTreeEntry => ({ type: "message", ...at(index), message: msg });
// The entry the embedded runner appends after each completed run (attempt-finalize.ts).
const bootstrapMarker = (index: number): SessionTreeEntry =>
  ({ type: "custom", customType: "openclaw:bootstrap-context:full", data: { timestamp: index }, ...at(index) }) as SessionTreeEntry;
const reply = (content: string, ts: number) =>
  assistant(content, { api: "openai-completions", provider: "stub", model: "m", timestamp: ts });
const big = (label: string) => `${label} ${"checklist line. ".repeat(250)}`;

describe("findCutPoint with a metadata entry before the kept user message", () => {
  it("does not report a split turn when the cut lands on a complete turn", () => {
    const withMarker = [
      message(user([text(big("turn 1"))], 1), 0),
      message(reply("reply 1", 2), 1),
      bootstrapMarker(2),
      message(user([text(big("turn 2"))], 3), 3),
      message(reply("reply 2", 4), 4),
      bootstrapMarker(5),
    ];
    const withoutMarker = withMarker.filter((entry) => entry.type === "message");

    // Control: same transcript without the marker cuts cleanly at the turn-2 user message.
    expect(findCutPoint(withoutMarker, 0, withoutMarker.length, 500)).toEqual({ firstKeptEntryIndex: 2, turnStartIndex: -1, isSplitTurn: false });
    // With the marker, the cut moves onto the marker and turn 1 becomes a "split-turn prefix".
    expect(findCutPoint(withMarker, 0, withMarker.length, 500)).toMatchObject({ isSplitTurn: false });
  });
});
```

Save it as `packages/agent-core/src/harness/compaction/compaction-cut-point.metadata-repro.test.ts`, then run:

```bash
node scripts/test-projects.mts packages/agent-core/src/harness/compaction/compaction-cut-point.metadata-repro.test.ts --maxWorkers=1
```

```text
- Expected
+ Received
  {
-   "isSplitTurn": false,
+   "isSplitTurn": true,
  }
```

The full result is `{ firstKeptEntryIndex: 2, turnStartIndex: 0, isSplitTurn: true }`, where index 2 is the marker.

**Gateway level.** This uses an isolated source Gateway with throwaway state and loopback stub providers. Seed eight `chat.send` turns, then send `/compact` with `agents.defaults.compaction: { mode: "safeguard", keepRecentTokens: 1000, qualityGuard: { enabled: false } }`. The primary stub returns 429 for summaries, so the fallback stub's request log shows each summary request.

| `contextInjection` | Marker entries | Fallback summary requests |
| --- | --- | ---: |
| default | yes | 2 (44,347 and 8,235 bytes; the second is the previous complete turn as a turn prefix) |
| `"never"` | no | 1 |

The harness and request logs are in [the evidence folder](https://github.com/saariuslystoned/openclaw/tree/5b8707883e2c7e38accb59455e146a178a78162f/pr-194813-gateway-validation/live-fallback), under `split-turn-dry-run/` and `dry-run/`.

### Expected behavior

A cut on the user message that starts a complete turn is not a split turn. `isSplitTurn` is false, and the safeguard makes one history summary request.

### Actual behavior

`isSplitTurn` is true, with `turnStartIndex` pointing at the previous turn. `turnPrefixMessages` holds that whole turn, and [the safeguard](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/1ad3ec3d2081f3c7228b5bed8ae95e3821cd609f/src/agents/agent-hooks/compaction-safeguard.ts#L944) makes a second `turn-prefix` summary request.

One possible direction is to decide `startsTurn` from the first message-bearing entry at or after the adjusted cut, not from the metadata entry the scan stepped onto. I have not written a fix.

### OpenClaw version

2026.11.1 (`1ad3ec3`, source checkout)

### Operating system

macOS 26.5.2

### Install method

Source checkout (`scripts/run-node.mjs gateway run`)

### Model

Any. Reproduced with loopback stub providers.

### Provider / routing chain

Stub primary (429 on summaries) to stub fallback, configured in `agents.defaults.model.fallbacks`.

### Impact and severity

Every Gateway session delivers bootstrap context by default, so any compaction whose cut lands on a user message pays one extra summary request. With the quality guard on, its retry doubles both. Each extra request costs provider quota. Nothing is lost: the extra section repeats content that is already summarized.

**Version:** OpenClaw 2026.10.10 (7dc3350), macOS 26.5.1, Apple silicon. Gateway under launchd.

**What happened**

The host lost power twice within two minutes (an undervoltage reset). The second reset killed the gateway right after it had created the WhatsApp credentials lock file (`~/.openclaw/credentials/whatsapp/default.lock`) and before the lock's JSON payload (`{pid, createdAt, starttime}`) reached disk, so the next boot found a 0-byte lock file. From then on every WhatsApp start waited the full lock timeout and exited with:

```
Another process owns this WhatsApp connection. | file lock timeout for .../credentials/whatsapp/default | file_lock_timeout
```

The channel auto-restart (10 attempts, 5 s to 169 s backoff) and the health monitor's restart every 10 minutes all hit the same wall. The channel stayed down for 39 minutes, until the empty file was moved aside by hand; it reconnected 3 seconds later. `lsof` showed no holder of the file at any point.

**Cause**

`src/infra/stale-lock-file.ts`, `shouldRemoveDeadOwnerOrExpiredLock` (checked on `main`):

```js
const payload = readLockFileOwnerPayload(params.payload);
if (payload?.pid) return isLockOwnerDefinitelyStale({ payload: params.payload });
if (!payload?.createdAt) return false;
const createdAt = Date.parse(payload.createdAt);
return !Number.isFinite(createdAt) || (params.nowMs ?? Date.now()) - createdAt > params.staleMs;
```

A payload with a `pid` is stale when that pid is dead or its start time changed; a payload with only `createdAt` is stale after `staleMs`. A payload with neither (what an empty or unparsable file reads as) returns `false`, so the lock is never stale and only a human can clear it.

**Expected**

An empty or unparsable lock file whose mtime is older than `staleMs` (or older than the current boot) counts as stale and is removed, the same way an expired `createdAt` does. Optionally log one line when that happens.

**Suggested fix (sketch)**

```js
if (!payload?.pid && !payload?.createdAt) {
  // empty or unparsable: fall back to the file's mtime
  return params.mtimeMs !== undefined && (params.nowMs ?? Date.now()) - params.mtimeMs > params.staleMs;
}
```

with the caller passing the lock file's `mtimeMs`. Writing the payload to a temp file and renaming it into place would also stop a crash from leaving an empty lock.

**Workaround in use meanwhile**

A local watchdog moves the lock aside when it is 0 bytes (or has no pid), older than the boot time, has no `lsof` holder, and the health output reports the WhatsApp channel disconnected with a lock error.

**Related (different failure modes, not duplicates):** #174895 (live owner judged stale), #162797 (WhatsApp `whatsapp_connection_owner_busy` against the gateway's own lock).

prs.body:
Fixes #193928

## What Problem This Solves

Fixes outbound text disappearing when a plugin passes a NaN limit to Markdown chunking helpers.

## User Impact

Plugin callers retain the complete text, ranges, and formatting instead of preparing an empty message. Ordinary first-party configuration already filters NaN; this fixes explicitly supplied SDK input. No migration or configuration change is needed.

## Why This Change Was Made

Treat NaN as unbounded at the existing chunk-limit normalization owner. Finite/fractional limits, Unicode boundaries, Infinity, and nonpositive limits keep their behavior. The separate render-aware fallback is unchanged.

## Evidence

- Two regressions fail on the base and pass with the repair: text/range/IR preservation, and real Zalouser send preparation with a synthetic transport. The latter retains `😀Hi é` with exact UTF-16 bold/italic offsets and exactly one send.
- The public `openclaw/plugin-sdk/text-chunking` assertion changes from NaN range ends/empty IR to complete styled text. An independent parent rerun on the committed source also passes.
- All six focused suites pass: `chunk-text`, `ir`, `render-aware-chunking`, SDK `text-chunking`, auto-reply `chunk`, and Zalouser `send` (186 tests). Touched lint/format, the package type graph, narrowed consumer typecheck, test-line ratchet, and diff checks pass.
- Independent parent source/proof review of `d622a2ba4cd872758e1fbc5cbd5036602cb1ecba..5843a3310d0fcbc0387cf3c05b9aee200a80e1c0` found no actionable issue. Author simplification keeps one NaN guard with no new abstraction or caller-side guard. Local autoreview/ClawSweeper was not run.

Limits: no live Zalo account, recipient delivery, full release build, or full-suite claim. The compiled CLI runtime-profile proof below is separate from the focused tests above. A broad typecheck selector was deliberately stopped after expanding to unrelated graphs; focused scopes passed. The documented SDK import-boundary inventory script is absent at the base; this change does not alter imports, exports, or topology.

Test cost: the 37 added test lines cover core and distinct consumer payload/style boundaries. Observed standalone wall time including compilation: 0.65 s core, 9.28 s Zalouser; CI timing unavailable. Production delta is +4/-1 lines.


---

# Compiled CLI behavior proof for PR #193937 / issue #193928

The real `openclaw.mjs` executable loaded a synthetic plugin through normal manifest/config discovery and `api.registerCli`. The registered command imported `openclaw/plugin-sdk/text-chunking` from built `dist` and wrote attributed outbound payloads to stdout. There was no source-loader command, test runner, transport mock, Gateway, account, or external send in this scenario.

Version: Node.js v24.22.1; OpenClaw 2026.11.1 (5843a33); pnpm 12.15.0; macOS arm64.
Fixed source: `5843a3310d0fcbc0387cf3c05b9aee200a80e1c0` (clean).
Negative control: a separate copy of that same head with **only the three-line NaN guard removed** from `packages/markdown-core/src/chunk-text.ts`, then rebuilt. It is a derived dirty negative control, not a claim to have checked out the pristine parent. The original PR worktree/head was unchanged.

## Actual build and entrypoint

Both copies ran the repository build profile:
```sh
node --import ./scripts/tsx.mjs scripts/build-all.mts cliStartup
```
Both exited 0. Fixed build: 146.8 s; negative-control build: 63.2 s (cache reuse). The CLI bootstrap import guard passed in both builds. This profile builds runtime JavaScript and CLI metadata; it is not a full release/declaration/UI build or an npm-published installation.

Each build was staged with `dist`, root launcher `.mjs` files, package metadata, built workspace packages, and physical dependency copies. The staged host had **no root `src` or `.git`**, preventing a source-checkout fallback. The fixture had a local `node_modules/openclaw` link to that staged host. Runtime invocation used the real package bin:
```sh
node <staged-package>/openclaw.mjs markdown-nan-proof
```
The command also checked that its SDK import resolved into `/dist/`. It supplied `const limit = Number.NaN` in plugin code, not JSON config; stdout confirms `typeof limit === "number"` and `Number.isNaN(limit) === true`.

## Sanitized terminal transcript

```text
$ node <fixed-package>/openclaw.mjs --version
OpenClaw 2026.11.1 (5843a33)
exit: 0

$ node <negative-control-package>/openclaw.mjs markdown-nan-proof
{"limitType":"number","limitIsNaN":true,"sdkResolvesToDist":true,"validRanges":false,"ranges":[{"start":0,"end":null}],"rangePayloads":[{"text":"","ranges":[]}],"irPayloads":[]}
Expected values to be strictly equal:

false !== true
exit: 1 (3.874 s)

$ node <fixed-package>/openclaw.mjs markdown-nan-proof
{"limitType":"number","limitIsNaN":true,"sdkResolvesToDist":true,"validRanges":true,"ranges":[{"start":0,"end":7}],"rangePayloads":[{"text":"😀Hi é","ranges":[{"start":0,"length":4,"style":"bold"},{"start":5,"length":2,"style":"italic"}]}],"irPayloads":[{"text":"😀Hi é","ranges":[{"start":0,"length":4,"style":"bold"},{"start":5,"length":2,"style":"italic"}]}]}
PASS: complete text and UTF-16 styles retained
exit: 0 (4.419 s)
```

JSON serializes the defective NaN range end as `null`; `validRanges: false` is computed before serialization using integer/bounds checks. Both source paths are actually executed before the assertions: ranges → slice IR → attributed renderer, and chunk IR → attributed renderer. The independent expected value requires exactly `😀Hi é`, a complete `[0,7]` UTF-16 range, bold offset 0 length 4, and italic offset 5 length 2.

## Reproduction fixture

Create `fixture/` with these three files. This plugin uses only public SDK imports and Node assertions; it introduces no production seam.

`fixture/package.json`:
```json
{
  "name": "markdown-nan-proof",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "type": "module",
  "openclaw": {
    "extensions": [
      "./index.mjs"
    ]
  }
}
```

`fixture/openclaw.plugin.json`:
```json
{
